    "body": "PARIS — Pan-European missile maker MBDA and German startup Atmos Space Cargo are teaming up to study deploying deep-strike weapons from Earth orbit using reentry vehicles.\nThe firms agreed to jointly assess the feasibility and pursue development of a precision orbital deep strike capability, combining military payloads with orbital transport and reentry vehicles, they said on Wednesday. The cooperation agreement will initially focus on system architecture, technical feasibility, payload integration and possible technology demonstrations, MBDA and Atmos said.\nAtmos CEO Sebastian Klaus has argued Europe needs an orbital deterrent to fill a capability gap in deep precision strike. He made the case in March for a conventional capability that can be deployed from orbit to strike any legitimate military target anywhere on Earth within 60 minutes.\n“The agreement brings together our expertise in orbital transportation and atmospheric reentry with MBDA’s defense systems expertise to explore a potential new conventional defense capability,” Klaus said in a post on social network LinkedIn on Wednesday. “Our objective is clear: deterrence. To help European allies prevent aggression by strengthening the credibility of Europe’s ability to defend itself.”\nThe companies are assessing how existing defense technology and new orbital technology can be combined to strengthen Europe’s conventional deterrence, Klaus said in a statement on Wednesday, calling orbital transportation and return “a new layer of strategic infrastructure.”\nAny future deployment and use would be subject to applicable law and decisions by the responsible governmental and military authorities, the startup said. Specific developments and future projects remain subject to further evaluation and separate agreements, according to Atmos.\nAtmos will contribute expertise in orbital transportation and reentry technology, with MBDA providing capabilities in defense systems and military payloads, the companies said. MBDA, which is owned by Airbus, BAE Systems and Leonardo, is Europe’s largest missile maker.\n“Our goal is to provide our armed forces with a highly innovative, sovereign European capability,” Thomas Gottschild, the managing director of MBDA Deutschland, said in a statement . “We are very much looking forward to this new project.”\nAtmos is developing a reusable spacecraft for low-Earth orbit cargo missions that uses an inflatable cushion as a heat shield and aerodynamic brake for reentry. The startup successfully tested the concept in April 2025, and is developing the Phoenix 2 orbiter with a payload capacity of 100 kilograms. The company said flight duration can range from 3 hours to 12 months.\nAtmos said its civilian Phoenix vehicles remain separate from the dedicated defense system being explored with MBDA.\nThe startup and the European Space Agency are also studying a larger version dubbed Phoenix 3 with a potential payload of about 1 metric ton.\nAtmos in April this year raised €25.7 million in a Series A funding round that it said would help finance an initial three-vehicle fleet of Phoenix 2 spacecraft as well as development of Phoenix 3. The firm also said it was setting up a business serving government and defense customers called Atmos Works.\nThe company last year set up an alliance with ARX Robotics to integrate unmanned systems with its reentry vehicle, envisaging using Phoenix to deliver autonomous systems to the Earth’s surface, including to support defense operations on the ground.",
